{"kind":"request","text":"Design follow-through from current complete reading of R1 a549aac6 sections 16,17,18,6.11,7.5 and9.2/9.3, with already completed6.4/6.6 and companion, plus current R4 8.6. Full new main reading is not claimed and no code is executed. Two concrete gaps concern the existing capacity/bounds promise and belong to R1/R4 with R3 declaring platform forms.\n\n1. Section17.1 counts entries AND retained inputs in Bytes, but17.2's byte reservation describes only the deciding form's fields/effects/sends/envelope and temporarily substitutes7.5's256KiB entry size. That bound does not cover the foreign entries, outcome evidence, detached text, rule inputs or ancestry snapshots that9.2/16 require newly retained. An entry's small uses list can name much larger retained bytes. A supported settling handler that reads two previously unretained foreign entries within their individual256KiB entry bounds can need more than the256KiB stand-in even while its own deciding entry is small; this is a design-bound witness, not a claimed execution failure of I1. The current statement that it always fits its own reservation is therefore not established by the entry-size surrogate.\nDefine the complete finite byte charge/reserve for newly retained judgment evidence and nested duties, alongside entry/item/record/send dimensions, before a duty is admitted. State shared/deduplicated input accounting and when bytes stored before the deciding entry become used/reserved or an independently owned temporary allocation; do not make retained evidence an uncharged cache or delete evidence/duties to fit. Snapshot/output/other evidence bounds and finite platform reservations must be actual owned decisions, not invented numeric quotas. Keep R4's numerical measurement ownership; a measured envelope allowance alone does not solve the semantic omission. Extend the existing capacity witness to show required retained inputs plus the deciding entry fit at capacity, without another heavyweight suite or per-field sweep. Preserve settlement/restart/source custody and full original outcomes.\n\n2. Section6.4 permits ReceiveType.class advisory with guards,effects,sends,attention and an opened item. Section6.6 gives handlers ordinary declared/attention sends. Yet7.5's per-kind table assigns delivery of an advisory zero sends, alongside control,diagnosis,preparation,settlement,checkpoint. No corresponding restriction of advisory handler forms is stated. Choose explicitly: if advisory handlers may emit their declared/attention sends, count/reserve those and preserve their lack of a platform result; if this class must emit none, make that a definition-admission restriction and explain how directory/inbox attention behavior is still supported. Do not silently drop a declared notification or send, and do not claim a zero-send row bounds a grammar that permits them. This is a documentation/protocol contradiction, not a claim that a particular existing demo lane has such a handler or a generic extra engine is needed.\n\nCombine both with current complete common/proof follow-through and ordinary independent findings; keep four current R1 subjects and the existing reviewer/route, no cancel/restart, standalone design note, stale adoption or extra source gate. Accepted base designs remain distinct from proposed new forms. Agreed I1 and highest-priority useful-test economy continue; builder judges self-host Jam readiness. Full cloud/browser/device experience, unknown-duty custody and all-component/application retarget/removal remain owed.","body":{"conditions":"Complete the byte-reservation formula for retained settlement evidence and reconcile advisory-handler sends with the per-kind bound table in the existing R1/R4 package. Preserve current ordinary review scope and full functional/removal outcomes; no new source gate or heavy suite.","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}